QA = [("Why do PMs need to know SQL vs NoSQL?", "Because the choice affects consistency, cost, speed and features. PMs should know SQL is best for money and orders, NoSQL for scale and flexibility, and most products use both."),
 ("Explain ACID to a non-technical person.", "It is four promises that make a transaction safe: all-or-nothing, rules always hold, simultaneous users do not interfere, and confirmed data survives a crash."),
 ("What is the CAP theorem?", "In a network split a system must pick between consistency (same truth everywhere, may refuse requests) and availability (always answer, maybe stale). Pick consistency for payments, availability for browsing."),
 ("What is Cassandra good at?", "Very high write volume, multi-region and always-on use cases like messages, feeds and event logs. It is weak at joins and strict transactions."),
 ("What is Paxos and why does it matter?", "An algorithm that lets servers agree on one value using a majority, even with failures. It underpins reliable leaders, locks and strongly consistent databases, at the cost of some latency."),
 ("Paxos vs Raft?", "Both achieve consensus. Raft is simpler to understand and implement, with a clear leader and elections; Paxos is the older, more general form."),
 ("How do you prevent selling the last item twice?", "Make check and decrement one atomic step, use holds with timeouts, add idempotency keys, and reconcile stock with vendors."),
 ("What is idempotency and why for payments?", "Repeating a request has the same effect as doing it once. It stops double charges when users retry or a payment callback arrives twice."),
 ("When would you use a vector database?", "For meaning-based search, RAG chatbots, recommendations and near-duplicate detection. Not for exact lookups."),
 ("What is RAG?", "Retrieval-augmented generation: fetch relevant documents (often via a vector DB) and let the AI answer using them, which reduces made-up answers."),
 ("Replication vs sharding?", "Replication copies the same data for safety and read speed; sharding splits different data across servers for capacity."),
 ("How would you estimate load for 1M orders a day?", "About 12 per second on average, 120 per second at a 10x peak, 2 GB of order data a day, and many more reads than writes."),
 ("What would you ask engineers before a sale?", "Expected peak requests per second, p99 latency, what fails first, load-test results, rollback plan and on-call coverage."),
 ("Monolith or microservices?", "Start simple; split services when teams and scale require it. Microservices bring speed for teams but add network, data and debugging complexity."),
 ("What is a Cassandra LWT?", "A lightweight transaction (IF NOT EXISTS or IF condition) that runs a Paxos round for a safe check-then-write. It is correct but slow, so use it only for rare, important actions like claiming a unique key."),
 ("What happens when traffic grows 20x?", "Add a load balancer and many identical stateless servers with autoscaling, cache hot data, add read replicas, move slow work to queues, rate limit, and switch off non-essentials. The next bottleneck is usually the database or a third-party API."),
 ("How do you stop duplicate orders?", "Use an idempotency key per checkout attempt, a unique database constraint on it, a short lock per cart, an order state machine and a disabled button. Replays return the same order."),
 ("Why is payment confirmed by webhook?", "The user may close the app or the screen may be wrong. The gateway tells your server the verified result, so the server decides, and reconciliation fixes any mismatch."),
 ("How do you avoid duplicate customers?", "A unique, normalised, OTP-verified phone number backed by a database constraint, a transactional check-then-insert, a documented merge process and promo limits."),
 ("What is a saga?", "A series of local transactions with compensating undo steps (refund, release stock) used instead of one giant distributed transaction.")]

